{'title': 'How do generative artificial intelligence (AI) tools and large language models (LLMs) influence language learners’ critical thinking in EFL education? A systematic review', 'doi': '10.1186/s40561-025-00406-0', 'year': 2025, 'venue': 'Smart Learning Environments', 'url': 'https://doi.org/10.1186/s40561-025-00406-0', 'source_db': 'openalex_search', 'populations': [], 'user_types': ['Student', 'Educator'], 'study_design': 'Meta-Analysis / Systematic Review', 'extended_summary': 'This paper addresses a timely question in education: whether generative AI tools and large language models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT support or undermine critical thinking in English as a Foreign Language (EFL) education. The authors note that prior work has focused mainly on technical benefits like grammar correction, vocabulary development, and writing support, while giving less attention to higher-order thinking. To address this gap, they conduct a systematic review focused specifically on critical thinking in EFL contexts.

Using the PRISMA framework, the authors searched Web of Science, Scopus, ProQuest, ERIC, and Google Scholar for studies published from 2022 to February 2025. After screening 3,243 records plus 4 additional sources, they included 15 studies. The review synthesizes evidence on the dual nature of generative AI and LLMs, the research methods used, the main thematic focuses, the theories and models applied, and the limitations and future directions in this research area. The reviewed studies concern EFL learners and teachers, with contexts including classroom teaching and language learning activities such as writing, speaking, argumentation, and literature learning.

The main conclusion is that generative AI and LLMs have both promise and risk for critical thinking in EFL education. Ten of the 15 studies (66.67%) reported positive effects, including improved argumentative writing and speaking, stronger analytical reasoning, better evaluation and problem-solving, increased engagement and confidence, and support for self-directed learning. At the same time, five studies (33.33%) warned that overreliance on AI could reduce analytical depth, creativity, autonomy, and independent reasoning. The authors conclude that AI can nurture critical thinking when used with guidance, critical evaluation, and pedagogical structure, but may hinder it when learners passively accept AI-generated content.', 'quality_tier': 'yellow', 'impact_tier': 'yellow', 'limitations': ['non_representative_population', 'implementation_fidelity_not_reported'], 'duration_weeks': 'not_reported', 'setting': 'not_reported', 'teacher_training': 'not_reported', 'implementation_fidelity': 'not_reported', 'study_country': 'not_reported', 'study_region': 'not_reported', 'extraction_status': 'full_text', 'extraction_note': 'Retrieved from KG corpus', 'interventions': [], 'outcome_assignments': [], 'authors': '', '_kg_source': True}
